<div class="docs-startup-page">

    <div class="docs-eyebrow">ENGINE / RUNTIME FLOW</div>

    <h1>
        Startup &amp; <span class="docs-accent">game loop</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        The executable receives a launch configuration, creates the window,
        initializes game APIs, constructs a scene, registers systems, and
        then updates and renders that scene.
    </p>

    <h2>Process entry point</h2>

    <p>
        <code>Crafty/Program.cs</code> expects a launch argument pair:
        <code>--config-path &lt;path&gt;</code>. The JSON file is deserialized
        into <code>GameLaunchConfigDto</code>, whose current documented
        payload contains <code>SelectedWorldPath</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The game registers GLFW windowing and input platforms, creates
        <code>MainWindow</code>, and activates it.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-code-card">
        <div class="docs-code-title">
            <span>LAUNCH CONFIGURATION</span>
            <span>JSON</span>
        </div>

        <pre><code>{
  "SelectedWorldPath": "C:\\Saves\\Example\\Example.world"
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h2>MainWindow initialization</h2>

    <p>
        The <code>MainWindow</code> constructor calls its initialization
        routine, creates a <code>WorldGenService</code> for the world
        directory, initializes <code>GameAPIs</code>, creates the player
        camera system, and subscribes to activation and focus events.
    </p>

    <p>
        When the window is activated, it constructs a <code>GameWorld</code>
        and an empty <code>Scene</code>. It then initializes the pause menu
        and HUD, creates player, camera, and hand objects, loads the world,
        connects the world's <code>BlockChanged</code> event to lighting
        and mesh invalidation, registers systems, and creates the job system.
    </p>

    <h2>System registration order</h2>

    <p>
        Systems are appended to <code>Scene.Systems</code> and updated in
        list order. The scene constructor inserts <code>HierarchySystem</code>
        first.
    </p>

    <p>
        <code>MainWindow</code> then registers world time, chunk streaming,
        hand animation, player and movement systems, gravity and collision,
        combat, damage, fall, death and respawn systems, inventory and
        hotbar synchronization, block interaction, mesh updates, and
        mob systems.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
        <i class="bi bi-info-circle"></i>

        <div>
            <strong>System order matters</strong>

            <p>
                A system sees the scene state left by systems that ran
                before it. When changing registration order, verify which
                systems produce state that later systems consume.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Per-frame execution</h2>

    <p>
        <code>GameWorld.Render(deltaTime)</code> iterates the registered
        systems. If the game is paused, systems implementing
        <code>IGameplaySystem</code> are skipped; ordinary
        <code>ISystem</code> implementations still run.
    </p>

    <p>
        After system updates, it finds the first object with a
        <code>Camera</code> component and passes the scene and camera
        to the renderer.
    </p>

    <h2>Game state and input</h2>

    <p>
        <code>GameStateManager</code> tracks <code>Paused</code>,
        <code>Playing</code>, and <code>Inventory</code> states.
        Escape toggles pause and resume while playing or paused.
    </p>

    <p>
        Window focus loss pauses the game unless the inventory or death
        state is active. The window switches between raw and normal
        cursor modes when entering or leaving gameplay.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-callout">
        <i class="bi bi-lightbulb"></i>

        <div>
            <strong>For engine contributors</strong>

            <p>
                When adding a system, decide whether it should continue
                while paused. Implement <code>IGameplaySystem</code> for
                simulation that must freeze with gameplay; implement
                <code>ISystem</code> for work that should continue under
                the current pause filter.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

